 'quoted_status_result': {'result': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1871415406012907742', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxMzU2MDE1MDI3ODYyNDAxMDI0', 'rest_id': '1356015027862401024', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': False, 'can_media_tag': False, 'created_at': 'Sun Jan 31 23:02:41 +0000 2021', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Asking questions, making connections.', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}, 'url': {'urls': [{'display_url': 'sensereceptornews.com', 'expanded_url': 'https://sensereceptornews.com/', 'url': 'https://t.co/T77ZF6x71h', 'indices': [0, 23]}]}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 146940, 'followers_count': 61848, 'friends_count': 5656, 'has_custom_timelines': True, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 324, 'location': 'Houston, Texas', 'media_count': 11473, 'name': 'Sense Receptor', 'normal_followers_count': 61848, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1819437546197180900'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/1356015027862401024/1734508291', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1864166506080169984/ob__WBbw_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': 'SenseReceptor', 'statuses_count': 62915, 'translator_type': 'none', 'url': 'https://t.co/T77ZF6x71h', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}, 'super_follow_eligible': True}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1871415406012907742'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1735018776000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '7239', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NzE0MTU0MDU2ODE1MjA2NDA=', 'text': 'Impossible to deny: "Vaccines" cause autism—rates are 4–5 times higher in "vaccinated" vs "unvaccinated."\n\n"We see this...consistent 4.55...[to] 5 times [increase] in autism [rate] in the studies that have considered fully vaccinated versus fully unvaccinated children."\n\nIn this clip from a recent Children\'s Health Defense (@ChildrensHD) town hall, Author of Vax-Unvax: Let the Science Speak and Chief Scientific Officer of Children\'s Health Defense Brian Hooker (@BrianHookerPhD) describes multiple studies that compared "vaccinated" children to "unvaccinated" ones and found that rates of autism were consistently between four and five times higher in the "vaccinated" cohorts. \n\nHooker even points out that in California—which has one of the most stringent "vaccine" policies in the U.S. re: children attending school—the rate of autism at this point is about 1 in 20 children; meanwhile, in a study Hooker himself (and others) conducted, the rate of autism in the "under vaccinated" (not even entirely "unvaccinated"!) was just 1 in 165. \n\nFurthermore, Hooker notes that this kind of relationship—with the "vaccinated" children suffering at a much greater rate than the "unvaccinated" ones—held true for other types of chronic diseases, including things like severe allergies, gastrointestinal disorders, asthma, and chronic ear infections. \n\n--------------Partial transcription of clip--------------\n\n"Let\'s look at this group of children, you know, this unique 7,600 group or children that we had in our study. And so right now in the United States, the current autism prevalence is 1 in 33 children. In California, it\'s about 1 in 20 children.\n\n"So, you know, average classroom has 1, 1 and a half autism, you know, upwards to 2 autism cases in every classroom in California. In our cohort for the clinical informatics program, the rate of autism in this unvaccinated and under vaccinated group was actually 1 in 165. So our incidence overall was much, much lower. The average number of vaccines, like I said, in the first year of life in this group was 7, okay? So they were under vaccinated compared to the national average, which presumably many, if not most children are receiving all of those vaccines in their first year of life, so upwards to 30 different vaccines through their first birthday.\n\n"And then in previous studies, we have seen that fully vaccinated children show a 5 times greater prevalence of autism than unvaccinated children. And here\'s a study that I did back in 2021 with Neil Miller, who\'s a medical journalist who\'s written about 7 different books on vaccines. And, this is actually I\'m sorry, this is not Hooker and Miller, this is the Anthony Mawson study from 2017. He showed that autism was about 4.2 times higher in fully vaccinated children than in unvaccinated children.\n\n"In the study that I did with Neil Miller, this is an earlier study in 2020, we showed that in partially vaccinated children versus unvaccinated children, developmental delays were twice as high. Asthma was 4.5 times as high. So this is consistent with the new results that I just presented.\n\n"The ear infections were about double. But then when you look at fully vaccinated children, these relationships jump off the chart. In fully vaccinated children versus unvaccinated children, this is a study that I did with Neil Miller in 2021. When we look at the red bars, fully vaccinated, severe allergies, 4.3 times higher, gastrointestinal disorders, 13.8 times higher, asthma, chronic ear infections, ADHD, all jump off the charts and then autism is still 5 times higher. So, you know, we see this sort of consistent 4.55, you know, 4.2 for the Mawson study, 5 times in autism in the studies that have considered fully vaccinated versus fully un unvaccinated children."', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '782018153207431168', 'name': 'Children’s Health Defense', 'screen_name': 'ChildrensHD', 'indices': [326, 338]}, {'id_str': '1668756944314863616', 'name': 'Brian Hooker, PhD', 'screen_name': 'BrianHookerPhD', 'indices': [466, 481]}]}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': [{'from_index': 361, 'to_index': 393, 'richtext_types': ['Italic']}]}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 33, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Tue Dec 24 04:39:36 +0000 2024', 'conversation_id_str': '1871415406012907742', 'display_text_range': [0, 279], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'media': [{'display_url': 'pic.x.com/fOvSpvOiyc', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/SenseReceptor/status/1871415406012907742/video/1', 'id_str': '1871410371006763008', 'indices': [280, 303], 'media_key': '7_1871410371006763008', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/ext_tw_video_thumb/1871410371006763008/pu/img/L2QePWgp3sfq1p1P.jpg', 'type': 'video', 'url': 'https://t.co/fOvSpvOiyc', 'additional_media_info': {'monetizable': False}, 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'sizes': {'large': {'h': 1080, 'w': 1920, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'medium': {'h': 675, 'w': 1200, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'small': {'h': 383, 'w': 680, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'thumb': {'h': 150, 'w': 150, 'resize': 'crop'}}, 'original_info': {'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'focus_rects': []}, 'allow_download_status': {'allow_download': True}, 'video_info': {'aspect_ratio': [16, 9], 'duration_millis': 187400, 'variants': [{'content_type': 'application/x-mpegURL', 'url': 'https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/1871410371006763008/pu/pl/Vm1XKIT-odoneEQw.m3u8?tag=12&v=ffd'}, {'bitrate': 256000, 'content_type': 'video/mp4', 'url': 'https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/1871410371006763008/pu/vid/avc1/480x270/1mPVTM3A3ptTjJoK.mp4?tag=12'}, {'bitrate': 832000, 'content_type': 'video/mp4', 'url': 'https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/1871410371006763008/pu/vid/avc1/640x360/kUjz3qvmreVRg21U.mp4?tag=12'}, {'bitrate': 2176000, 'content_type': 'video/mp4'